      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we need to break down the problem into smaller steps.\n\n1. **Identify the possible outcomes for the first die:**\n   - The dice have six faces, numbered from 1 to 6.\n   - The numbers less than three are 1 and 2.\n   - Therefore, there are 2 favorable outcomes for the first die: 1 and 2.\n   - The probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{first die} < 3) = \\frac{\\text{number of favorable outcomes for the first die}}{\\text{total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Identify the possible outcomes for the second die:**\n   - The numbers greater than three are 4, 5, and 6.\n   - Therefore, there are 3 favorable outcomes for the second die: 4, 5, and 6.\n   - The probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{\\text{number of favorable outcomes for the second die}}{\\text{total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the combined probability:**\n   - Since the outcomes of the first die and the second die are independent events, the probability of both events occurring (rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die) is the product of their individual probabilities:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and second die} > 3) = P(\\text{first die} < 3) \\times P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
