      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we can break down the problem into smaller steps.\n\n1. Identify the possible outcomes for each die:\n   - A number less than three on the first die: The possible outcomes are 1 or 2. So, there are 2 favorable outcomes for the first die.\n   - A number greater than three on the second die: The possible outcomes are 4, 5, or 6. So, there are 3 favorable outcomes for the second die.\n\n2. Determine the total number of possible outcomes when rolling two dice:\n   - Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of possible outcomes when rolling two dice is \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\).\n\n3. Calculate the number of favorable outcomes:\n   - The number of favorable outcomes (rolling a 1 or 2 on the first die and a 4, 5, or 6 on the second die) is the product of the number of favorable outcomes for each die: \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\).\n\n4. Determine the probability:\n   - The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes: \\(\\frac{6}{36} = \\frac{1}{6}\\).\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
