      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we can break the problem down into two separate events and then combine their probabilities.\n\n1. **Probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die:**\n   The first die is a six-sided die, and the numbers less than three are 1 and 2. So, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{first die} < 3) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die:**\n   The second die is also a six-sided die, and the numbers greater than three are 4, 5, and 6. So, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Combining the probabilities of the two independent events:**\n   Since the events are independent, the probability of both events happening is the product of their individual probabilities.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and second die} > 3) = P(\\text{first die} < 3) \\times P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
