      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the possible outcomes for each die:**\n   - A six-sided die has numbers {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}.\n   \n2. **Determine the favorable outcomes for each die:**\n   - For the first die, the number less than three is {1, 2}.\n     \\[\n     \\text{Number of outcomes for the first die} = 2\n     \\]\n   - For the second die, the number greater than three is {4, 5, 6}.\n     \\[\n     \\text{Number of outcomes for the second die} = 3\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when rolling two dice:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of outcomes} = 6 \\times 6 = 36\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the number of favorable outcomes:**\n   - The number of outcomes where the first die shows a number less than three and the second die shows a number greater than three is:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 3 = 6\n     \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the probability:**\n   - The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes.\n     \\[\n     \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{6}{36} = \\frac{1}{6}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
